# Bring the CDC-NCM+ACM gadget up when a UDC appears.
#
# A UDC only exists when the Type-C port lands device-role, i.e. when felix is
# cabled to a host/PC rather than to the dongle. usb-gadget.service's
# WantedBy=multi-user.target only fires at boot, but the cable gets moved
# between the dongle (ethernet + charging) and the PC (gadget + fastboot)
# while the machine is running -- so trigger on UDC hot-plug as well.
#
# Safe for the host-mode path: with the dongle attached the port is data=host,
# dwc3 exposes no UDC, this rule never matches, and the service's
# ConditionPathExistsGlob=/sys/class/udc/* would no-op it anyway.
SUBSYSTEM=="udc", ACTION=="add", TAG+="systemd", ENV{SYSTEMD_WANTS}="usb-gadget.service"

# ...but the udc rule above is NOT sufficient on its own. The UDC node does not
# come and go with the cable: /sys/class/udc/11210000.dwc3 exists whenever dwc3
# is loaded, in host mode too (measured on 34291FDHS000WV while it was on its
# dongle). So "udc add" fires at driver load and essentially never again —
# moving the cable from the dongle to a PC produces no udc event at all.
#
# This was masked until the data_role guard landed: usb_gadget used to bind
# unconditionally at boot, so the gadget existed no matter the cable. Now a
# dongle boot correctly declines, and without a second trigger nothing ever
# starts it when the cable later moves to a host.
#
# Type-C partner attach DOES fire on every cable change. Triggering on it is
# safe because usb_gadget re-checks data_role itself and no-ops in host mode.
SUBSYSTEM=="typec", ACTION=="add", KERNEL=="port0-partner", TAG+="systemd", ENV{SYSTEMD_WANTS}="usb-gadget.service"
